    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d618">

                <pb facs="1182" n="1164" sameAs="frus:frus1951v06p1/pg1164"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">694.001/6–2851: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        United States Political Adviser to SCAP
                    </hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Sebald</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-06-29">June 29, 1951</date>, 12
                        noon.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Topad 1848. For Allison from Dulles. Reurtel
                    2255, June 28.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d618-f1"/> It is present assumption as
                    working hypothesis Peace Treaty and bilateral security treaty will not come into
                    force prior end Korean hostilities. <pb facs="1183" n="1165" xml:id="pg1165"/>
                    If, however, conditions next year seemed to make desirable put peace treaty into
                    effect prior ending hostilities consideration wld be given to consummating
                    arrangement with Jap along lines of previously discussed addendum<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d618-f2"/> providing for continuing use of Jap facilities. In this
                    connection consideration wld surely be given to position of other Allied Powers
                    participating in hostilities. It can be assumed that govts of Allied Powers
                    whose troops are actively participating in Korean hostilities wld consult to
                    prevent timing of treaty coming into force which wld embarrass common effort.
                    Foregoing your background for informal and strictily confidential discussions
                    with friendly Allies who raise questions as reported in urtel. [Dulles.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d618-f1" n="1" xml:id="d618-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d618-f2" n="2" xml:id="d618-f2-content">
                <p>For the last mention of this “addendum”, see the attachment to Mr. Dulles’
                    memorandum to the Secretary of April 25, <ref target="#pg1019">p.
                    1019</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
